opic 5 — Combining Lists + Conditional Rendering

Yesterday's lesson and today's lesson often work together.

Example:

function App() {
  const products = [];

  return (
    <div>
      {products.length > 0 ? (
        products.map((product) => (
          <p key={product.id}>{product.name}</p>
        ))
      ) : (
        <p>No products available.</p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;

Let's understand the condition:

products.length > 0

means:

Does the array contain at least one item?

If yes:

Render products

If no:

No products available.

Flow:

products
   ↓
products.length > 0?
   │
   ├── YES
   │    ↓
   │   map()
   │    ↓
   │   Product list
   │
   └── NO
        ↓
   "No products available"

This pattern is everywhere in real applications.



10. Interview Questions
Q1. How do you render a list in React?

Usually by using JavaScript's map() method.

users.map((user) => (
  <p key={user.id}>{user.name}</p>
))
Q2. What is a key in React?

A key is a stable identifier React uses to distinguish sibling elements in a rendered list.

Q3. Why are keys important?

They help React correctly match list items between renders when items are added, removed, reordered, or updated.

Q4. Can two elements have the same key?

Sibling elements in the same list should have unique keys.

Q5. Should we use array index as key?

Avoid it for dynamic lists when a stable unique ID exists.

Q6. Why is map() preferred over forEach() for rendering?

Because map() returns a new array, which can contain JSX elements.

Q7. Is key passed as a normal prop?

This is a very good interview question.

No.

Suppose:

<ProductCard
  key={product.id}
  name={product.name}
/>

Inside ProductCard, you cannot access it as:

props.key

If the component itself needs the ID, pass it separately:

<ProductCard
  key={product.id}
  id={product.id}
  name={product.name}
/>

Now:

props.id

is available.

